.TH tar 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
tar \- the tape archiver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tar
.BR \-c | \-t | \-x
[\fB\-vnk\fR] [\fB\-f\fI archive\fR] [\fB\-b\fI blocks\fR] [\fIfile\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tar
packs a set of files, with their directories, into one archive, and
takes them out again.  The format is the ordinary one, so an archive
made here can be unpacked on any other Unix and the other way round.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c
Create an archive from the named files.
.TP
.B \-t
List what is in an archive.
.TP
.B \-x
Extract from an archive.
.TP
.BI \-f " archive"
Use this file rather than standard input or output.
.TP
.B \-v
Say what is being done, file by file.
.TP
.B \-n
Do not restore ownership.
.TP
.B \-k
Keep existing files rather than overwriting them.
.TP
.BI \-b " blocks"
Blocking factor.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# tar -cvf /mnt/backup.tar /root
# tar -tf backup.tar
# tar -xvf backup.tar
.fi
.SH NOTES
There is no compression: no
.BR \-z ,
no
.BR \-j .
An archive is exactly as large as what went into it, rounded up.
.PP
Extracting an archive made elsewhere can create paths longer than this
filesystem is happy with, and file names are limited to 100 characters
by the format itself.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR uue (1),
.BR fat (1),
.BR cp (1)
